<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Quiets the console output the CLI writes while tests run.

  This matters more here than in NOpenNLP.Tools.Tests. Writing to the console is
  the CLI's whole job: every tool prints its usage, its progress, its results or
  its errors, and the end-to-end tests drive real tools over real corpora. On
  top of that the tools inherit the library's own progress and diagnostic
  output, so a single test can emit a training log.

  The VSTest MSBuild task surfaces anything a test writes to stderr as a build
  *warning*, attributed to Microsoft.TestPlatform.targets rather than to this
  repo, which makes routine tool output read like build problems. Since these
  tests assert on what the tools print, they capture it deliberately via
  Console.SetOut/SetError and then assert on the captured text; letting the
  adapter also forward it to the runner adds nothing.

  Suppressing at this layer rather than in the ported code keeps every
  Console.Out/Console.Error call matching its Java counterpart, which is what
  makes the next 1.9.x rebase cheap.

  Applied automatically. The test csproj points RunSettingsFilePath here, so a
  plain "dotnet test" picks it up, CI included. To see the full console output
  for a run, pass the adapter setting NUnit.ConsoleOut=1 as a runner argument,
  after a bare double-dash separator on the dotnet test command line.
-->
<RunSettings>

  <NUnit>
    <!--
      Stops the NUnit adapter forwarding what a test writes to Console.Out and
      Console.Error into the runner output. A failing test is unaffected: its
      assertion message, stack trace and own standard output are still reported,
      so nothing is lost when debugging a failure.
    -->
    <ConsoleOut>0</ConsoleOut>
  </NUnit>

</RunSettings>
